<section id="idea">
  <div class="col">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>The central idea</h2>
    <p>Most cataloguing tools assume one file is one item. Sheet music breaks that assumption immediately: a CD-ROM rip puts sixty pieces in one PDF, and an archive puts one concerto across eight. So the schema separates three things that other designs collapse into one.</p>
  </div>

  <pre class="mermaid">
graph LR
    C[Collection<br/>a directory + its adapter] --> F[SourceFile<br/>one PDF on disk]
    F --> P[Piece<br/>a page range = one entry]
    P --> W[Work<br/>the music itself]
    W --> K[Composer]
    P -.-> FC[FieldCandidate<br/>every signal, kept]
    style P fill:#1F4E5F,color:#fff
    style W fill:#E3ECEE
  </pre>

  <div class="col">
    <p><strong>Piece</strong> is the unit you browse: a page range inside a file, with its own title, composer and confidence. <strong>Work</strong> is the music, independent of how many copies you hold — it carries the canonical links, because an IMSLP page describes a sonata, not your particular scan of it. <strong>SourceFile</strong> is bookkeeping about a PDF: size, hash, page count, where its library copy lives.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="invariant">
    <span class="tag">The invariant everything else protects</span>
    <p>A human decision permanently outranks a machine one. An accepted candidate short-circuits its field entirely: no combination of signals, no re-scan, no enrichment pass can replace it. Every feature that writes to the catalogue has to be checked against this.</p>
  </div>
</section>

